A||N||7||MrFeeArrow||Wind shield wipers||||1||
Z||000000||MrFeeArrow||02-13-2000||12:15 AM||||My wind shield wipers have a mind of there own when it gets cold here in florida.<BR>They stay on when selected off? Then after a few miles of travel they work OK? <BR>?????????????????????||||reg||1
Z||000001||Ed||02-13-2000||01:15 AM||||Might have something to do with the circuit board inside your wiper module in the front compartment.  Has it been raining? My Fiero and my work S-10 both have had wiper run-on when the module gets wet. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||stimpy||02-13-2000||09:54 AM||||My wipers did this for a while, now they never shut off.  I had to fabricate (like that word? <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">) a pushbutton switch to the fusebox to control them.  My ? is how can I tell if it is a circuit board or the wiper control stalk. Do I need to get a new board or will salvage yard units work ok?||||reg||1
Z||000003||wkayl||02-13-2000||10:12 AM||||I switched out the delay wiper controll and module from 1 car to another (had just gotten 2nd Fiero the day before).  When I turned on the key the wipers ran abour 4 or 5 wipes without even touching the switch.  When I set them on delay they would keep running.  I swapped out the wiper motor and that fixed the problem.  The moral of this story: Your problem may be the wiper motor circut that returns the blades to the down position.||||reg||1
Z||000004||mutfish||02-13-2000||10:15 AM||||Cold?.....Florida? yeah right......||||reg||1
Z||000005||burdell1||02-13-2000||12:27 PM||||Take the circuit board out of the wiper motor. Check to see if there are any burned up circuits. This is what happened to mine. However, after I replaced the circuit board, it would work fine for awhile then it would burn out again. I think that the wiper switch might be burning out the circuit board. Any ideas anyone?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Phaeton||02-13-2000||01:40 PM||||If the wipers won't quit it is the park switch on the end of the motor.  Sometimes they stop and sometimes they don't is gunk on a switch contact. Single speed operation usually is the lever assembly.  Burning circuit boards is most likely a ricer curse, light four black candles, one by each corner, pour a bottle of warm saki over the wiper assembly while singing America the Beautiful.  This will alert the neighbors and perhaps one of those nice persons in the white coats will know how to find the leaky wiring. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||mwbackus||02-13-2000||02:47 PM||||My wiper blades did the ghost thing after I had washed out the front compartment.  I painted the wiper arms red (color matched to exterior) and they have not done this since.  Of course, I have not washed the front comparment since then either, but I am sure that is just a coincidence.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Mike||||reg||7
